  <section class="section teal-soft">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="reveal">How to tell which route fits</h2>
      <p class="reveal">The three routes are not ranked. Which one applies depends on three things you can answer before a buyer sees anything.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>How defined the records are</strong>A named set of systems, years and fields can go to a buyer as a package. Exports with no boundary are not yet something anyone can license.</li>
        <li><strong>Whether one buyer is enough</strong>One license is simpler to negotiate. Running several buyers in parallel is slower, and it is the only way to learn whether the first offer was fair.</li>
        <li><strong>Who holds the copy afterward</strong>A direct license and a package both leave the buyer with the records. An introduction keeps the decision with you until the terms are signed.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="reveal">A company can change route partway through. What it should not do is license the same records twice on terms that contradict each other, which is why exclusivity is worth reading before price.</p>

      <h2 class="reveal">What does not happen</h2>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>No public listings</strong>Your data is not posted for anyone to bid on.</li>
        <li><strong>No handover before approval</strong>Nothing moves until you have agreed the buyer and the terms in writing.</li>
        <li><strong>No price on this page</strong>Value depends on the data, the rights and the terms. We discuss it on a call.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="reveal">Where a route stalls</h2>
      <p class="reveal">A route rarely stalls on price alone. More often the records cannot be handed over in the form a buyer needs, and that is cheaper to find out before samples are shared.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>The work was never written down</strong>Advice given on calls and decisions kept in people&rsquo;s heads leave nothing to license, however valuable the experience behind them was.</li>
        <li><strong>The records cannot be reproduced</strong>If the system was replaced and the history did not move with it, there is no dataset left to describe.</li>
        <li><strong>The right to license is unclear</strong>Client confidentiality, personal data or a tool&rsquo;s own terms can rule out records that look like a good fit, which is why <a href="is-my-customer-data-sellable.html">personal data is checked first</a>.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="reveal">What the end of a license can look like</h2>
      <p class="reveal">A license ends, but a model that has already learned from the data does not forget it. That is why the end of the term is usually written as three separate things rather than one.</p>
      <ul class="checklist reveal">
        <li><strong>Copies of the data</strong>Deleted or returned within a set period, and confirmed in writing.</li>
        <li><strong>Models already trained</strong>The terms decide whether they may keep being used, sold or further trained once the term ends.</li>
        <li><strong>New training</strong>No new model is trained on the data after the term unless the license is renewed.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

